An arrow after an index still needs the element loaded.

THIS IS THE FAULT THE STAGE-3 SPIKE HAS BEEN CHASING. With it fixed, micro-c's
tcc compiles a translation unit and writes a valid object file, with no
workaround of any kind:

    exit=0
    /tmp/o.o: ELF 64-bit LSB relocatable, ARM aarch64, version 1 (SYSV)

WHAT IT IS. postfix_expr_array blanked the element load in every lvalue
context, `&` among them:

    if (... || match(".", tok) || is_prefix_operator
            || is_postfix_operator || was_address_of)
        assign = "";

Every other entry there is right: in an lvalue context the ADDRESS of the
element is the answer and loading it goes one step too far. An arrow is not
like the others. The element is a POINTER, and the thing being addressed is at
the other end of it, so the load is not optional -- it is how you get there.

    ps = &table_ident[v - TOK_IDENT]->sym_identifier;        tccgen.c

`&` set was_address_of, the load was blanked, and the member offset was added
to the address of the ARRAY SLOT rather than to the TokenSym it points at.

THE ARITHMETIC IS EXACT, which is how it was finally pinned. sym_identifier
sits at offset 32 in TokenSym, so the write lands four slots further along a
table of eight-byte entries:

    alloca            v - TOK_IDENT = 195     corrupted slot 199
    __builtin_alloca  v - TOK_IDENT = 1086    corrupted slot 1090

Both off by exactly 4. global_identifier_push then stored a Sym* into each --
over a live entry of tcc's own token table -- on every declaration carrying an
asm rename, of which tcc's predefs have about twenty.

The crash was three removes from that: the overwritten entries later reach
tal_free as pointers belonging to no allocator buffer, and tal_free_impl walks
its buffer list off the end with no NULL check.

SAME RULE, THIRD FUNCTION. EXPERIMENT-zzc taught it to postfix_expr_arrow and
zzg to postfix_expr_dot: a request for an address applies to the LAST step of a
chain, never to a step that still has to be followed. postfix_expr_array had to
learn it separately. That is four functions now implementing one rule -- the
class MICRO-C.md counts -- and it is the strongest argument in the file for
making the rule live in one place.

HOW LONG IT TOOK, AND WHY. This bug survived a great many rounds behind
instrumentation that was itself broken. Recorded so the next hunt does not
repeat it:

  * a watch that cached a slot ADDRESS and went stale when the array moved
  * a `static` detector called from another file, so it silently never ran
  * a watch that RESET its baseline on the first hit, after which every later
    probe compared against the corrupt value and reported "clean"
  * a print of a field taken before the field was populated
  * PREDEF_LINES and KEYWORD_LIMIT switches that did nothing for two rounds
    because getenv returned NULL -- which was EXPERIMENT-zzl, an unrelated
    linkage bug, silently disabling the instruments

Three "ruled out" conclusions had to be retracted, and the correct
localisation (predef line 95) was found early, dismissed on the strength of a
switch that was not working, and then found again. If a probe reports silence,
check that it can still speak.

WHAT IT REVEALS NEXT. A real program now gets a DIAGNOSTIC rather than a
signal:

    /tmp/real.c:1: error: internal compiler error: vstack leak (1)

That message is legible only because zzl gave tcc a working stderr. tcc's own
self-checks are reporting for the first time, which is a different and much
better regime to debug in.

MEASURED

    difftest      65 pass / 0 fail / 1 known gap, both architectures
    corpus        423 of 426 on aarch64
    vocabulary    clean on five architectures
    mc-tcc -c     empty unit -> valid aarch64 ELF relocatable, exit 0
                  real unit  -> tcc diagnoses a vstack leak

